{"st":"IL","name":"Illinois","seats":[{"census":1810,"seats":1},{"census":1820,"seats":1},{"census":1830,"seats":3},{"census":1840,"seats":7},{"census":1850,"seats":9},{"census":1860,"seats":14},{"census":1870,"seats":19},{"census":1880,"seats":20},{"census":1890,"seats":22},{"census":1900,"seats":25},{"census":1910,"seats":27},{"census":1920,"seats":27},{"census":1930,"seats":27},{"census":1940,"seats":26},{"census":1950,"seats":25},{"census":1960,"seats":24},{"census":1970,"seats":24},{"census":1980,"seats":22},{"census":1990,"seats":20},{"census":2000,"seats":19},{"census":2010,"seats":18},{"census":2020,"seats":17}],"events":[{"congress":15,"year":1818,"kind":"statehood","title":"Statehood","text":"Illinois became the 21st state on December 3, 1818. Congress gave it 1 seat in the House until the next apportionment.","sources":["https://www.everycrsreport.com/reports/R47747.html","https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":18,"year":1822,"kind":"census","title":"1820 census apportionment","text":"After the 1820 census, Illinois kept 1 seat in the House. It elected its one member statewide (at-large).","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":23,"year":1832,"kind":"census","title":"1830 census apportionment","text":"After the 1830 census, Illinois's House delegation grew from 1 to 3 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":28,"year":1842,"kind":"census","title":"1840 census apportionment","text":"After the 1840 census, Illinois's House delegation grew from 3 to 7 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":33,"year":1852,"kind":"census","title":"1850 census apportionment","text":"After the 1850 census, Illinois's House delegation grew from 7 to 9 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"census","title":"1860 census apportionment","text":"After the 1860 census, Illinois's House delegation grew from 9 to 14 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":38,"year":1862,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 38th through 42nd Congresses (1863–1873), Illinois elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":43,"year":1872,"kind":"census","title":"1870 census apportionment","text":"After the 1870 census, Illinois's House delegation grew from 14 to 19 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":48,"year":1882,"kind":"census","title":"1880 census apportionment","text":"After the 1880 census, Illinois's House delegation grew from 19 to 20 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"census","title":"1890 census apportionment","text":"After the 1890 census, Illinois's House delegation grew from 20 to 22 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":53,"year":1892,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 53rd Congress (1893–1895), Illinois elected 2 of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":58,"year":1902,"kind":"census","title":"1900 census apportionment","text":"After the 1900 census, Illinois's House delegation grew from 22 to 25 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"census","title":"1910 census apportionment","text":"After the 1910 census, Illinois's House delegation grew from 25 to 27 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":63,"year":1912,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 63rd through 77th Congresses (1913–1943), Illinois elected 2 of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":73,"year":1932,"kind":"census","title":"1930 census apportionment","text":"After the 1930 census, Illinois kept 27 seats in the House.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"census","title":"1940 census apportionment","text":"After the 1940 census, Illinois's House delegation shrank from 27 to 26 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":78,"year":1942,"kind":"at-large","title":"Some members elected at-large","text":"In the 78th through 80th Congresses (1943–1949), Illinois elected one of its representatives statewide (at-large) and the rest by district.","sources":["https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://history.house.gov/Institution/Apportionment/Apportionment/"]},{"congress":79,"year":1946,"kind":"court","title":"Colegrove v. Green","text":"Illinois voters challenged congressional districts that had not been redrawn since 1901 and had very unequal populations. The Supreme Court upheld the dismissal of the case; the lead opinion called districting a political question.","sources":["https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/328/549"]},{"congress":83,"year":1952,"kind":"census","title":"1950 census apportionment","text":"After the 1950 census, Illinois's House delegation shrank from 26 to 25 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":88,"year":1962,"kind":"census","title":"1960 census apportionment","text":"After the 1960 census, Illinois's House delegation shrank from 25 to 24 seats.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf"]},{"congress":93,"year":1972,"kind":"census","title":"1970 census apportionment","text":"After the 1970 census, Illinois kept 24 seats in the House. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":98,"year":1982,"kind":"census","title":"1980 census apportionment","text":"After the 1980 census, Illinois's House delegation shrank from 24 to 22 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":103,"year":1991,"kind":"court","title":"Hastert v. State Board of Elections","text":"Illinois fell from 22 seats to 20 after the 1990 census, and the legislature missed its deadline without passing a plan. On November 6, 1991, a three-judge federal court ordered use of a plan proposed by the members of Congress who had sued.","sources":["https://case-law.vlex.com/vid/hastert-v-illinois-state-888052035","https://www.mnsenate.gov/departments/scr/REDIST/Redsum/ilsum.htm"]},{"congress":103,"year":1992,"kind":"census","title":"1990 census apportionment","text":"After the 1990 census, Illinois's House delegation shrank from 22 to 20 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":105,"year":1998,"kind":"court","title":"King v. State Board of Elections","text":"Voters challenged the court-drawn 4th District as a racial gerrymander. A federal court found race predominated but upheld the district as needed to comply with the Voting Rights Act, and the Supreme Court affirmed in 1998.","sources":["https://www.mnsenate.gov/departments/scr/REDIST/Redsum/ilsum.htm"],"mapChange":false},{"congress":108,"year":2002,"kind":"census","title":"2000 census apportionment","text":"After the 2000 census, Illinois's House delegation shrank from 20 to 19 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":113,"year":2012,"kind":"census","title":"2010 census apportionment","text":"After the 2010 census, Illinois's House delegation shrank from 19 to 18 seats. Because districts must have nearly equal populations, the map was redrawn with the new census counts.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/376/1"]},{"congress":118,"year":2021,"kind":"other","title":"Legislature passes 17-district map","text":"After Illinois dropped to 17 seats, the legislature passed a new congressional map on November 1, 2021. The governor signed it November 23.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/illinois/"]},{"congress":118,"year":2022,"kind":"census","title":"2020 census apportionment","text":"After the 2020 census, Illinois'