linois's seats from 14 to 19. It was first used in the 1872 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":19},"c048":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1880 census, which raised Illinois's seats from 19 to 20. It was first used in the 1882 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":20},"c054":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1890 census, which raised Illinois's seats from 20 to 22. It was first used in the 1894 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":22},"c058":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1900 census, which raised Illinois's seats from 22 to 25. It was first used in the 1902 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":25},"c073":{"why":"at-large","kind":"at-large","text":"Illinois kept its existing districts and still elected 2 members statewide (at-large).","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators"],"districtsChanged":0},"c081":{"why":"researched","kind":"other","text":"Before the 1948 election Illinois redrew its districts, which had largely dated from 1901, and replaced its statewide (at-large) seat with a new district, for 26 districts in all.","sources":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1948_United_States_House_of_Representatives_elections","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Illinois%27s_at-large_congressional_district","https://github.com/unitedstates/congress-legislators","https://www.law.cornell.edu/supremecourt/text/328/549"],"secondary":true,"districtsChanged":26},"c083":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1950 census, which cut Illinois's seats from 26 to 25. It was first used in the 1952 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":8},"c088":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1960 census, which cut Illinois's seats from 25 to 24. It was first used in the 1962 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":22},"c090":{"why":"researched","kind":"court","text":"In People ex rel. Scott v. Kerner the Illinois Supreme Court held the 1961 districts violated the equal-population rule of Wesberry v. Sanders. After the General Assembly adjourned without a new plan, the state and federal courts approved a provisional court-drawn map for 1966.","sources":["https://hallapproved.com/il/cases/supreme/1965/2244218"],"districtsChanged":23},"c093":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1970 census, which left Illinois with 24 seats. It was first used in the 1972 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":24},"c098":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 1980 census, which cut Illinois's seats from 24 to 22. It was first used in the 1982 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":24},"c103":{"why":"event","kind":"court","text":"New map following: Hastert v. State Board of Elections. Illinois fell from 22 seats to 20 after the 1990 census, and the legislature missed its deadline without passing a plan. On November 6, 1991, a three-judge federal court ordered use of a plan proposed by the members of Congress who had sued.","sources":["https://case-law.vlex.com/vid/hastert-v-illinois-state-888052035","https://www.mnsenate.gov/departments/scr/REDIST/Redsum/ilsum.htm"],"districtsChanged":22},"c108":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2000 census, which cut Illinois's seats from 20 to 19. It was first used in the 2002 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/decennial/2000/data/apportionment/2000%20PHC3%20Table%203.pdf","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":20},"c113":{"why":"census","kind":"census","text":"New map drawn after the 2010 census, which cut Illinois's seats from 19 to 18. It was first used in the 2012 election.","sources":["https://www2.census.gov/programs-surveys/decennial/2020/data/apportionment/apportionment.csv","https://cdmaps.polisci.ucla.edu/"],"districtsChanged":19},"c118":{"why":"event","kind":"other","text":"New map following: Legislature passes 17-district map. After Illinois dropped to 17 seats, the legislature passed a new congressional map on November 1, 2021. The governor signed it November 23.","sources":["https://redistricting.lls.edu/state/illinois/"],"districtsChanged":18}}}